 ⚡️ Situational awareness: President Trump said today that he has called for a ban on U.S. diesel exports, joining other conservative voices who back the policy. Go deeper . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing — AI&#x27;s next frontier: Cheaper power 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios AI companies have competed on having the best and often most dangerous models for years. Now they're pivoting to a new focus: cost, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 

 🎉 Driving the news: It's modelpalooza in the AI world this week, with new releases from OpenAI, Anthropic, SpaceX and some Chinese providers.

 Most of them are offering shocking levels of intelligence at prices that would've been unthinkable just a few months ago. Top companies are finding a variety of ways to make powerful systems more efficient — for example, by using smaller portions of a model for some answers. Zoom in: OpenAI is releasing GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna today, which bring down the cost for top business customers by 50% from previous iterations.

 Anthropic is releasing Opus 5.5 today, which the company says costs around 40% less to run than Opus 5 while maintaining top intelligence. Elon Musk's xAI yesterday released Grok 4.7, pitching the new model around price-performance. Yet in a testament to the sheer brutality of competition in the AI race, it only took 24 hours for new options to wipe out much of that price advantage. Follow the money: A recent Citadel Securities report found that falling costs are fueling more use, and that overall AI spending is increasing. 

 That points to higher eventual profits for AI labs and related companies. The bottom line: AI sticker shock feels like a distant memory, given the massive push to deliver cheaper models.

